ing wall  works moving on to our fifth and final  detail let's close out of that one  we'll go ctrl c let's simply just copy  this section across another three meters  and move back up to our floor plan what  i'll do is select my column tool  let's go to a steel column we'll open up  all the settings so we can go through  it it'll be an 89 by 89 millimeter steel  column that's a traditional  column here in australia again all sorts  of different  metal steel across the world we'll put a  wall thickness in there just for fun  let's call it four millimeters it's  very thick all we'll do now is click ok  and drop that column directly in the  middle of our section  now still columns are very simple detail  once again and they're most likely going  to be detailed by a structural engineer  unless there's an architectural feature  around there that is critical just like  all of the rest of these details  they will be predominantly provided by  the structural engineer  it's our job then to go and enhance them  from an architectural point of view  but without knowing the fundamental  basics of how this works  you won't be able to do anything  architecturally speaking  so opening up this sectional view with  open current view settings  we can see our column coming straight  through and let's go ahead  and draw a nice footing here like we did  for the retaining wall itself so let's  draw a 600 by 300 footing  let's drag that to the center of our  column right here  and then what i'm going to do let's drop  that 25  mil for the instance now basically what  would happen  with this steel column to the footing  connection  is we would have a base plate that would  look something along  the lines of this i'll draw it very  mediocre  just so we can get an understanding of  what that base plate would look like  and then we would have a little bit of  grout  filled in underneath this section of  that base plate  so the grout would be on top of that  concrete footing  then there would 